{#- The page for somebody who has finished Duolingo Hebrew, or a course like it, at
    `/after-duolingo-hebrew` (targum-internal#676, step 3; David, 2026-11-10).

    The end of a course is a moment people search for, so this page answers it, drawn as
    the front page is (design's spec, 2026-11-12): the front page's own box to try a link
    of theirs beside a word card, a real first text in the reader under both, why the
    Hebrew of a course and the Hebrew of a video feel so different (shown, in the How
    cards' pictures), and three graded first texts as the Library draws them. Like
    /aliyah it carries no script, so everything is drawn in the markup. Its one
    ask is the front door's, the waitlist or Sign up, and the form says it was pressed
    here (`from=after-duolingo-hebrew`).

    Duolingo is named (David lifted "competitors never named" for this audience on
    2026-11-10), and the page says once, in its questions, that targum is not affiliated
    with it. Nothing on it is Duolingo's: no logo, colour or character, and no list of
    their words is marked known.

    English only. Its lines are written here rather than through the catalogue: a key in
    `en.json` with no Russian beside it fails `test_strings.py`, and a Russian edition is
    ling-ru's to write, so the page waits for one rather than carrying half of each.

    Drawn from the front door's parts and held to its rules (§12, 2026-10-30): every file
    baked in or named on targum's own origin, nothing from anybody else's. -#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
{% include '_public_head.html.j2' %}
{{ styles('landing.css', 'shelf.css') }}
{{ hebrew_face() }}
{{ chrome_face() }}
</head>
<body class="landing">
<a class="skip" href="#top">{{ t("landing.page.skip-to-the-page", "Skip to the page") }}</a>
{#- The bar's places are the front door's, so `home` is its address. Its RU goes to the
    Russian front page: this page has no Russian yet. Sign in and Sign up carry where the
    visitor came from. -#}
{% with bar_class = 'bar', home = '/', faq = '#questions', here = '/', join = '#join', signin = '/account/signin?lang=en&from=' ~ came_from %}{% include '_front_bar.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}

<main id="top">
  {#- The front page's top (design's spec, 2026-11-12): the hero, the word card beside it
      where the front page's card stands, and the reader the full width under both. Drawn
      in the markup, since the page carries no script: the card is open on the word the
      reader's third line has open, and nothing drawn here is a press that does nothing. -#}
  <div class="col top">
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="headline">
        <p class="label">After Duolingo</p>
        <h1>You finished Duolingo Hebrew. Now learn from real Hebrew.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Paste a link to a Hebrew video or article and read it with English beside every line and the vowels whenever you want them.</p>
        {% include '_try_box.html.j2' %}
        <p class="try-else">Nothing to paste yet? Start with one of our <a href="#firsts">first texts</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="card" id="card" aria-label="{{ t("landing.page.word-card", "Word card") }}">
      <div class="card-head">
        <span class="label">{{ t("landing.page.word-card", "Word card") }}</span>
        <p class="card-word" lang="he" dir="rtl">בֵּית סֵפֶר</p>
        <p class="card-sense">school <span class="pos">· {{ t("landing.card.noun-masculine", "noun, masculine") }}</span></p>
      </div>
      <dl class="facts-list">
        <dt>{{ t("landing.card.here", "Here") }}</dt>
        <dd><span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְ</span> + <span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">בֵית־הַסֵּפֶר</span><span class="here-gloss">to school</span></dd>
        <dt>{{ t("landing.card.plural", "Plural") }}</dt>
        <dd><span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">בָּתֵּי סֵפֶר</span></dd>
        <dt>{{ t("landing.card.from", "From") }}</dt>
        <dd><span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">בַּיִת</span>, house, and <span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">סֵפֶר</span>, book</dd>
      </dl>
    </aside>

    <div class="showcase">
      {#- Four lines of a real first text (`yatziv-shoelace-seller`, public domain), as the
          library points them, with the English under each. A story, not a recording, so
          no line carries a time. -#}
      <article class="reader still" aria-label="Example: The Shoelace Seller in the reader">
        <div class="reader-head">
          <div>
            <p class="reader-title">The Shoelace Seller</p>
            <p class="reader-meta">A story · 3 minutes · A1</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lines">
          <div class="pair">
            <p class="line-he" lang="he" dir="rtl">– בֶּן כַּמָּה אַתָּה, שְׁלֹמֹה?</p>
            <p class="line-en">“How old are you, Shlomo?”</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pair">
            <p class="line-he" lang="he" dir="rtl">– בֶּן תֵּשַׁע – הוּא עוֹנֶה בְּקוֹל נָמוּךְ וְעֵינָיו מֻשְׁפָּלוֹת.</p>
            <p class="line-en">“Nine,” he says quietly, looking down.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pair now">
            <p class="line-he" lang="he" dir="rtl">– מַדּוּעַ אֵינְךָ הוֹלֵךְ <mark class="w open">לְבֵית־הַסֵּפֶר</mark>?</p>
            <p class="line-en">“Why don’t you go to school?”</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pair">
            <p class="line-he" lang="he" dir="rtl">– אַבָּא שׁוֹלֵחַ אוֹתִי לִמְכֹּר שְׂרוֹכִים.</p>
            <p class="line-en">“Dad sends me to sell shoelaces.”</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        {%- if linked and story %}
        <p class="reader-foot"><a href="{{ story.href }}">Read it in the library</a></p>
        {%- endif %}
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="part" id="why">
    <div class="col">
      <div class="part-head">
        <p class="label">After a course</p>
        <h2>Why real Hebrew feels harder</h2>
        <p>A course builds every sentence around words you’ve just practised. A news clip or a TV show doesn’t wait for you, so Hebrew you already know can look new.</p>
      </div>
      {#- The front page's How cards, each with a picture that shows its point, so the
          words under each heading can be few. -#}
      <ul class="how four">
        <li class="how-card">
          <h3>Most Hebrew has no vowels.</h3>
          <p>Here you can turn them on for the whole text whenever you need them, and off again when you don’t.</p>
          {#- The line as written and the line pointed. Still, they stand one under the
              other; with motion allowed they share one place and the vowels come and go
              on the written line every few seconds (landing.css, `.vowels-vis`). -#}
          <div class="how-vis vowels-vis">
            <div class="vs-as">
              <p class="label">As written</p>
              <p class="he vs-line" lang="he" dir="rtl">מדוע אינך הולך לבית־הספר?</p>
            </div>
            <div class="vs-with">
              <p class="label">With vowels</p>
              <p class="he vs-line" lang="he" dir="rtl">מַדּוּעַ אֵינְךָ הוֹלֵךְ לְבֵית־הַסֵּפֶר?</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="how-card">
          <h3>Small words get glued on.</h3>
          <p>“And”, “the”, “in” and “to” join the front of the next word, so <span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְבֵית־הַסֵּפֶר</span> doesn’t look like <span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">בית ספר</span>. Tap it and you see the word you already know.</p>
          <div class="how-vis center">
            <p class="he how-word" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְבֵית־הַסֵּפֶר</p>
            <ol class="glued" dir="rtl">
              <li><span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">לְ</span><span>to</span></li>
              <li><span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">בֵית</span><span>house of</span></li>
              <li><span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">הַ</span><span>the</span></li>
              <li><span class="he" lang="he" dir="rtl">סֵּפֶר</span><span>book</span></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="how-card">
          <h3>People talk fast.</h3>
          <p>Videos and podcasts are written out word by word and timed to the voice, so each word lights up as it’s said.</p>
          {#- The front page's own example video, its 2:19 line, one word lit as it's said. -#}
          <div class="how-vis">
            <div class="pair now">
              <span class="at tnum">2:19</span>
              <p class="line-he" lang="he" dir="rtl">עַכְשָׁיו שׁוֹבְרִים אֶת הַבֵּיצִים <span class="w saying">יְשִׁירוֹת</span> לְתוֹךְ הָרֹטֶב.</p>
              <p class="line-en">{{ t("landing.demo.now-crack-the-eggs-straight-into-the-sauce", "Now crack the eggs straight into the sauce.") }}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="how-card">
          <h3>There’s no list to finish.</h3>
          <p>Read anything you like. We keep count of every word you learn, and each text tells you how much of it you already know.</p>
          {#- The front page's own Your Progress figure (its "Watch your Hebrew grow"). -#}
          <div class="how-vis">
            <div class="how-count"><span class="v">2,140</span><span>{{ t("landing.page.words-known", "words known ·") }} {{ t("progress.where.most.article", "You'd follow most of a news article") }}</span></div>
            <div class="how-bars"><i class="h30 on"></i><i class="h42 on"></i><i class="h54 part"></i><i class="h66"></i><i class="h78"></i><i class="h90"></i></div>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {#- Three real texts from the library, measured A1 or A2 (`AFTER_DUOLINGO_FIRSTS`),
      drawn as the signed-out Library draws a row (`shelf.css`), three across. -#}
  <section class="part" id="firsts">
    <div class="col">
      <div class="part-head">
        <p class="label">First texts</p>
        <h2>Start with one of these.</h2>
        <p>All three are in the library, free to read.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="texts lib-rows">
        {%- for first in firsts %}
        <li class="lib-row">
          {#- The whole card opens the text's page in the library where the library is open
              to strangers; where it is not, it is named without a link that would end at a
              refusal. -#}
          {%- if linked %}
          <a href="{{ first.href }}">
          {%- else %}
          <div class="lib-name">
          {%- endif %}
            <bdi class="lib-title" lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ first.hebrew }}</bdi>
            <span class="english" lang="en" dir="ltr">{{ first.english }}</span>
          {%- if linked %}
          </a>
          {%- else %}
          </div>
          {%- endif %}
          <span class="byline">{% if first.level %}{{ first.level }} · {% endif %}{{ first.kind }} · {{ tn("landing.try.minutes", first.minutes, "{n} minute", "{n} minutes") }}</span>
          <span class="blurb">{{ first.blurb }}</span>
        </li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="part" id="questions">
    <div class="col">
      <div class="part-head">
        <p class="label">{{ t("landing.page.questions", "Questions") }}</p>
        <h2>{{ t("landing.page.before-you-join", "Before you join.") }}</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <details><summary>Will targum know the words I learned on Duolingo?</summary><p>Not by itself. When you join, we show you some Hebrew words and you mark the ones you know.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do I need to finish the whole course first?</summary><p>No. If you can read the letters and know a few hundred words, the A1 texts are a good place to start.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is targum part of Duolingo?</summary><p>No. targum isn’t affiliated with Duolingo. We built it for learners who have finished a course like it.</p></details>
        <details><summary>{{ t("landing.page.what-will-it-cost", "What will it cost?") }}</summary><p>{{ t("landing.page.the-library-is-free-to-read-and-stays", "The library is free to read and stays free. There’ll be a paid plan for reading your own Hebrew: the videos, podcasts and books you bring us.") }}</p></details>
        {% if not door_is_open() %}
        <details><summary>{{ t("landing.page.when-does-it-open", "When does it open?") }}</summary><p>{{ t("landing.page.we-re-opening-in-small-groups-rather-than", "We’re opening in small groups. Join the waitlist and we’ll email you when your turn comes.") }}</p></details>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {%- if not signed_in|default(false) %}
  {% include '_join_end.html.j2' %}
  {%- endif %}
</main>

{% with signin = true %}{% include '_public_foot.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}
</body>
</html>
